<title>
<![CDATA[ Improving Retrieval of Canopy Chlorophyll Content by Integrating Leaf Spectral Measurements Into SAIL Model ]]>
</title>
<link>
<![CDATA[ http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11434857 ]]>
</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ Canopy chlorophyll content (CCC) is a critical biochemical indicator of vegetation growth status and photosynthetic capacity, with broad applications in global climate change monitoring, agricultural productivity assessment, and ecosystem management. The PROSAIL model has been extensively applied for remote sensing retrieval of CCC; however, it shows notable limitations in accurately simulating canopy reflectance within the red-edge spectral region (680–780 nm), which in turn reduces the reliability of CCC estimation. To overcome this limitation, this study proposes a novel canopy spectral simulation approach, termed the measured-leaf-driven SAIL (mSAIL) model, which incorporates measured leaf spectra into the SAIL model to improve the spectral simulation accuracy of the red-edge region. First, measured leaf spectra from the ANGERS dataset (a widely used laboratory-measured leaf spectral dataset) were incorporated into the SAIL model to simulate mSAIL canopy reflectance under a range of leaf and canopy parameter conditions. Subsequently, the mSAIL-simulated spectra were validated using field measurements and compared with the corresponding PROSAIL simulations. Finally, empirical statistical models were constructed using the mSAIL and PROSAIL simulated spectra to retrieve CCC. Validation using field measurements collected in 2002, 2004, and 2006 indicates that mSAIL performs substantially better than PROSAIL in simulating red-edge reflectance, with corresponding improvements in the accuracy of red-edge vegetation indices (VIs). Furthermore, in empirical CCC retrieval applications, mSAIL consistently outperformed PROSAIL across all validation years. The three-year combined validation results show that mSAIL achieved root mean square error (RMSE) reductions of 43.53%, 29.17%, and 15.18% for MERIS Terrestrial Chlorophyll Index (MTCI), red-edge chlorophyll index (CIRE), and ND705, respectively. In summary, the mSAIL approach establishes a practical hybrid framework that effectively improves the accuracy of vegetation canopy spectral simulations, particularly in the red-edge region, thereby providing strong support for precise retrieval of CCC. ]]>
</description>
