</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ Building height mapping is widely coupled with footprint segmentation in a unified multitask learning (MTL) framework. In this study, we comprehensively explore the designs of the shared encoder, the bottleneck, and the decoders’ cross-task feature sharing in MTL for precise building height mapping, and propose Mamba-based building height estimation network (MBHNet). First, we discovered that Mamba, a state space model (SSM) variant with selective feature extraction capacity, is particularly suited for inferring building heights. However, in previous remote sensing (RS) related studies, only the global feature correlation capacity, the same as Transformer, is utilized. We design a local-then-global feature encoder, locally sensitive SSM (LS-SSM), which integrates neighborhood attention (NATTEN), input discriminant enhancer (IDE), and spatial-dimension selective SSM, ensuring rich local-to-global information extraction and strong adaptability for MTL tasks. Second, a channel-dimension selective SSM (CSSM)-based bottleneck is introduced to model interchannel dependencies, in which, unlike previous works, the channel-wise information is not compressed and fully preserved. Third, considering the redundancy of the commonly used full feature sharing strategy across different tasks, we develop a K-top relevant feature mining (KTM) strategy in the decoding stage to enable selective and pruned cross-task feature transfer. Extensive experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate that MBHNet consistently outperforms recent methods, achieving very significant relative gains of 12.7%, 32.7%/40.2%, and 32.8% in mean absolute error (MAE) for building height estimation on the GeoNRW dataset, Tainjin Test Set-I/II, and the ISPRS Potsdam dataset, respectively. The datasets used in this study will be made publicly available at: https://github.com/yudawen/MBHNet ]]>
