<description>
<![CDATA[ Multiobject tracking (MOT) in satellite videos is pivotal for global and dynamic object monitoring. However, the movable objects such as airplanes, ships, and cars in satellite videos are usually small, with ambiguous margins and features. It is challenging to identify and associate the same objects between adjacent frames, especially for those of the same class. To capture the distinguishing features among objects across consecutive frames, we propose a triple contrastive learning tracker (TCLTracker) based on contrastive learning (CL). In order to more accurately locate the objects with confused margins and small size, we design the identity CL (ICL) to minimize the feature variance between predicted and ground-truth boxes of each object and meanwhile varying the features of different objects to capture the unique features for each object. To accurately associate each object with similar appearance across frames, we propose the cross-frame CL (CFCL) to align the features between adjacent frames of the same object and increase the feature distance between different objects. The ICL and CFCL boost feature discriminability, but object features from the same class still need to maintain a level of correlation. Thus, the correlation-guided CL (CGCL) employs a linear combination of other object features to reconstruct each object feature. By aligning reconstructed features with the corresponding feature of ground-truth bounding box as positive pairs, the discriminability and correlation of the extracted features are effectively balanced. Furthermore, we adopt multidimensional trajectory strategy (MTS), making full use of motion prediction and feature similarity information for more accurate association. Experiments on satellite video datasets, including AIR-MOT, SAT-MTB, and VISO, validate the effectiveness and state-of-the-art performance of our proposed method. ]]>
