<description>
<![CDATA[ Moving infrared small target detection (MIRSTD) is crucial for various civilian applications. Motion information plays a key role in detecting and locating moving targets in complex infrared scenarios. Existing methods have primarily focused on designing and optimizing robust, complex 3-D spatial–temporal modules to process multiple consecutive input frames to extract motion information, yet they have largely overlooked the description of motion and optimization of input strategies, which limits their efficiency. In this work, we propose a novel motion-aided spatial–temporal integrated network (MIST-Net). We first optimize the input strategy by encoding the dominant motion characteristics across multiple consecutive frames using series expansion and temporal motion intensity analysis, thereby constructing a motion frame that consolidates motion information from several frames into one. The motion frame and target frame are then fed into the integrated network for extracting motion and spatial features, respectively. Additionally, to account for the mutual constraints and dependencies between motion and spatial information, we introduce a mutual calibration module (MCM) that refines the motion maps using spatial cues and enhances the spatial maps with motion information. To further improve the robustness of MIST-Net, we address the semantic gap between motion and spatial features by presenting a motion-spatial-coupled selective state space module (MSCM). Specifically, we generate a motion-spatial coupled state based on the mutual dependencies between the motion hidden state and spatial motion state using entropy theory. This coupled state dynamically adjusts the fusion of motion and spatial information. We conduct extensive experiments on the public SIRST and ITSDT-15K datasets, and the results demonstrate that MIST-Net outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods in both accuracy and speed. ]]>
