<description>
<![CDATA[ Land surface temperature (LST) is a key parameter for characterizing the surface energy state and plays a crucial role in agriculture, ecology, and climate research. In recent years, the demand for high-spatiotemporal resolution and continuous LST data has been rapidly increasing. Geostationary satellite observations offer great potential due to their high temporal resolution, but coarse spatial resolution and extensive cloud contamination limit their use. Most existing studies on geostationary satellite LST are limited to a single task, focusing solely on either spatial downscaling or gap filling, which fails to produce continuous high-resolution datasets. In this study, we propose a novel method for reconstructing hourly ~1-km all-weather (AW) LST based on FY-4A geostationary satellite observations, which simultaneously achieves the spatial resolution enhancement and reconstruction within a unified framework. Specifically, a kernel-driven model constrained by the diurnal temperature cycle (DTC) is first developed to conduct the spatial downscaling and reconstruct the ideal LST. Subsequently, a hybrid neural network integrating meteorological and radiative drivers is employed to infer weather-induced perturbations. Ground validation demonstrates that the method achieves high accuracy and robust performance across different times, land surface types, and cloud cover conditions (root-mean-square error (RMSE): 2.53–4.24 K, mean absolute error (MAE): 2.00–3.27 K, and R ${}^{\mathrm {^{2}}}$ : 0.92–0.95). This study not only provides a new approach for high-spatiotemporal resolution AW LST reconstruction from geostationary satellites but also offers valuable data support for related research fields. ]]>
