<description>
<![CDATA[ Ground-penetrating radar (GPR) diffractions typically manifest weak responses resulting from electromagnetic wave interactions with subwavelength heterogeneities (e.g., cracks and voids) or geometric discontinuities (e.g., scattering edges and corners). These subtle yet informative wavefield components are critical for enabling enhanced illumination and dedicated imaging of subsurface microfeatures in infrastructure assessment and geological hazard mitigation. Current diffraction extraction methods in GPR face significant practical limitations: kinematic local-slope techniques are computationally intensive and may lead to loss of vertex events, whereas coherence analysis (CA) requires meticulous calibration of scaling factors and time-moveout corrections. Such constraints can compromise the resolution of imaging modalities, such as migration, tomography, and full-waveform inversion (FWI). To address these challenges, this study introduces an adaptive diffraction extraction workflow that integrates three core modules in wavelet and dip domains: a wavelet-based reflection model for structural preservation, dip-adaptive smoothing operators for wavefield decomposition, and particle swarm optimization (PSO) with multiobjective criteria (Laplacian sharpness, wavelet entropy (WE), and focusing metrics) for parameters optimization. The workflow iteratively extracts residual diffractions by optimizing wavelet decomposition levels, smoothing scales, and residual thresholds using PSO-driven fitness functions. Validated through the heterogeneous random media model, the Sigsbee2a benchmark, and field experiments, the PSO-guided workflow demonstrates quantifiable improvements in edge preservation, coherence artifacts reduction, spatial resolution, and kinematic consistency. By prioritizing the wavefield integrity and structural fineness, the proposed method provides refined inputs for subsequent imaging, highlighting its potential to advance the resolution and reliability of near-surface GPR sensing applications. ]]>
