<description>
<![CDATA[ Spaceborne bistatic frequency-modulated continuous wave (FMCW) synthetic aperture radar (SAR) faces several challenges, including the nonlinear motion trajectory of the satellite platform, intrapulse motion of the transmitting and receiving satellites, space-variant characteristics of echo signals, and the breakdown of azimuth shift invariance. The “stop-and-go” hypothesis is no longer applicable in spaceborne bistatic FMCW SAR. In this article, an extended ground Cartesian back-projection (EGCBP) algorithm for bistatic FMCW SAR is proposed under the “non-stop-and-go” hypothesis. First, to address the peak positioning deviation of the range migration curve caused by intrapulse modulation effects, the least squares method is employed to perform range-dimensional linear fitting of the signal propagation delay, thereby obtaining the theoretical peak position of the range migration curve under the “non-stop-and-go” hypothesis. Second, since the traditional ground Cartesian back-projection (GCBP) algorithm is applicable only to monostatic systems, the spectrum compression function is enhanced to accommodate bistatic systems. Prior to this, to overcome the high computational cost of echo simulation using the iterative method under the “non-stop-and-go” hypothesis, a signal propagation delay approximation method (SPDAM) is introduced. This method not only meets accuracy requirements but also significantly improves simulation efficiency, providing a foundation for subsequent verification of the imaging algorithm. Simulation experiments involving both point and scene targets are provided to demonstrate the effectiveness and potential of the proposed algorithms for practical applications. ]]>
