<title>
<![CDATA[ 3SP-DUNet: An Interpretable Deep-Unfolded Network With Spatial–Spectral Sparse Priors for Hyperspectral Pansharpening ]]>
</title>
<link>
<![CDATA[ http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11435167 ]]>
</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ Most existing deep learning-based pansharpening methods adopt typical black-box models to achieve the fusion of panchromatic (PAN) and low-resolution hyperspectral (LRHS) images. This type of architecture has limited interpretability, making it difficult to ensure the fidelity of spatial and spectral features in the fusion results. To address this issue, we propose an interpretable deep unfolding network with spectral–spatial sparsity priors (3SP-DUNet), which is designed based on the unfolding optimization algorithm for solving the HS pansharpening problem. First, the objective function for solving high-resolution hyperspectral (HRHS) images in the HS pansharpening task is optimized to obtain the optimized solution formula. Then, based on the solution terms in the formula, spectral degradation/reconstruction (SpeD/SpeR) blocks and spatial degradation/reconstruction (SpaD/SpaR) blocks are constructed to simulate the degradation and reconstruction process of spectral and spatial features. Regarding the prior regularization terms in the formula, spectral/spatial sparse prior blocks are designed to constrain the solution space, ensuring the fidelity of spectral and spatial features. In addition, to reduce feature loss caused by increasing network depth, a cross-stage memory fusion unit (CMFU) is constructed to supplement features by fusing reconstructed images from adjacent stages. To guide the model to reconstruct accurate spectral features, a spectral similarity loss is defined by calculating the cosine values of the corresponding spectral vectors between the fused image and the corresponding ground-truth (GT) image. Extensive experiments demonstrate that 3SP-DUNet surpasses some state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods in both quantitative metrics and visual quality, while offering superior interpretability. The source code of this work will be released on GitHub. ]]>
