</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ In hyperspectral imagery, intraclass heterogeneity often arises from local environmental variations and spatial distribution nonuniformity, leading to significant discrepancies across spectral and spatial dimensions and limiting the capacity of unified modeling mechanisms. The Mamba model, built upon the selective state-space model (SSM), integrates local perception with long-range dependency modeling; however, its state evolution matrix adopts a single evolution pattern, lacking dynamic adaptability to heterogeneous features. To address this limitation, we propose a unified framework that integrates multipattern state evolution (MPSE) and dual-path spectral–spatial (DP-SS) modeling. MPSE introduces multiple learnable evolution patterns for hidden states, enabling each sample to dynamically integrate diverse historical information and thereby capture intraclass spectral–spatial heterogeneity. Meanwhile, DP-SS models feature along both spectral and spatial paths, enhance the representation of spectral trends and spatial structures, and fuse the two paths for adaptive global feature integration. The synergy of these modules strengthens the model’s ability to discriminate intraclass heterogeneous land covers. Experiments conducted on a self-constructed UAV hyperspectral dataset of the Yellow River Wetland demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms existing approaches, achieving an overall accuracy (OA) of 98.27%, an average accuracy (AA) of 98.84%, and a Kappa coefficient of 98.63%, offering a new perspective for efficient and fine-grained hyperspectral image classification. ]]>
