<description>
<![CDATA[ YOLO, as a representative real-time object detection model, is widely used in remote sensing tasks. However, due to its general-purpose design, it struggles to detect small and dim objects in remote sensing scenarios. While recent improvements to YOLO have focused on improving accuracy, they often overlook computational costs, which limits their deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. To address these challenges, we propose adaptive dual imitation learning (ADIL), a novel strategy designed to enhance the feature representation of small and dim objects in remote sensing scenes. ADIL equips the YOLO detector with a feature imitation (FI) branch, which is discarded during inference to maintain efficiency. Specifically, ADIL combines self-imitation and VLM-imitation learning strategies, each constructing exemplar sets to guide the feature learning process. In self-imitation learning, high-quality samples are dynamically selected based on the network’s learning state to build the exemplar sets. In VLM-imitation learning, text-space features from a vision-language model are used to construct the exemplar sets. Additionally, a contrastive learning-based imitation loss is applied to optimize the imitation branch. When integrated into YOLO, ADIL significantly improves detection performance on multiple remote sensing datasets while preserving the original speed of the detector, achieving a better balance between accuracy and efficiency. We hope that this approach provides valuable insights for real-time remote sensing object detection. ]]>
