</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ Accurate 3-D reconstruction of satellites from satellite-to-satellite (STS) optical imagery is crucial for space situational awareness (SSA), yet it is significantly challenged by the extreme space environment. Conventional 3-D Gaussian splatting (3DGS) pipelines degrade when facing STS-specific challenges, particularly in surface reconstruction quality. To overcome these obstacles, we propose Satellite-GS, a robust and precise STS reconstruction framework built upon enhanced 2D Gaussian splatting (2DGS), which enables high-quality surface reconstruction with fewer parameters. Initially, we introduce a satellite image deblurring (SID) model to perform motion deblurring and restore texture details. Subsequently, the 2DGS enhancement introduces key innovations: KNN-based floater removal (KFR) eliminates outliers and a pixel-enhance (PE) loss that preserves unlit satellite structures from erosion under extreme radiance imbalance. Moreover, we integrate an iterative 2DGS super-resolution (I2DSR) that amplifies fine-grained detail while maintaining multiview consistency, and employ an adaptive binary-quaternary splitting (ABQS) strategy that selectively densifies low-texture regions for detail refinement. We further introduce the NASA3D-Blur dataset, a specialized benchmark for SID evaluation, and conduct a comprehensive evaluation on the deblurred NASA3D-MVS multiview reconstruction dataset, comprising 30 real-world satellites captured in STS scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Satellite-GS substantially outperforms existing baselines, delivering superior texture fidelity under the harsh conditions of spaceborne relative imaging. ]]>
