<description>
<![CDATA[ Adapting large-scale vision foundation models (VFMs), pretrained on natural images (NIs), to remote sensing (RS) downstream tasks such as domain adaptation semantic segmentation (DASS) through parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) is a promising yet challenging direction. However, due to the large domain gap between NI and RS images (RSIs), as well as the inherent intradomain variations in the RS DASS task, existing PEFT methods struggle to achieve satisfactory adaptation. To address these challenges, we propose Bridge, a framework with small trainable parameters specifically designed for RS DASS. Bridge introduces a set of learnable domain-invariant tokens (DITs) that capture transferable knowledge shared across NI and RSI domains. To complement this, a spatial prior module (SPM) is proposed to extract multiscale spatial priors that provide auxiliary guidance for DIT adaptation (DITA), which produces domain-specific tokens. Finally, domain-aware injection (DAI) injects the domain-specific tokens into intermediate VFM features, aligning them with RS distributions and facilitating effective DASS task adaptation. With significantly fewer trainable parameters, Bridge efficiently transfers NI-pretrained VFMs to the RS DASS task. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Bridge substantially outperforms state-of-the-art PEFT approaches. Remarkably, compared with the current SOTA, Bridge achieves a significant improvement in mIoU. The code will be released at Bridge. ]]>
