</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ Viscoacoustic least-squares reverse time migration (QLSRTM) has emerged as a cutting-edge imaging tool, which can overcome the intrinsic attenuation effects of subsurface media and provide a robust solution for reconstructing the true reflectivity of subsurface. However, the migrated image of QLSRTM generally have side-effects mainly caused by the inaccurate background velocity model, incomplete acquisition geometry, inadequate physical modeling assumptions, and so on. Due to the excellent edge-preserving denoising capability, the total variation (TV) norm regularization has been introduced into the least-squares inversion framework to suppress the side-effects and give a segmented smooth solution. Nevertheless, conventional TV norm constraint solely considers the gradient variation of inversion parameter along the horizontal and vertical directions, which might reduce its adaptability for complex structures. For this reason, we propose a QLSRTM algorithm that integrates a structure-oriented TV norm regularization into the objective function. Specifically, the decoupled fractional Laplacians (DFLs) viscoacoustic wave equation is utilized to accurately characterize the propagation effects of seismic waves in subsurface media. Besides the structural directional information are extracted from the inverted image through gradient structure tensor (GST) method within each iteration and then incorporated into the conventional TV norm to constraint inversion. In this article, we adopted the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMMs) to iteratively solve the constraint structure-oriented TV norm minimization problem. The feasibility and effectiveness of the proposed approach are validated through two synthetic and a field data. Migration results demonstrate that the proposed approach can not only compensate for attenuation effects effectively and minimize the migration artifacts significantly, but also obtain a better quality for imaging complex structures than conventional regularization. ]]>
