<![CDATA[ Regional Gravity Field Modeling Using Two-Cumulative SRBFs Approach for the Realization of Geopotential-Based Height System ]]>
</title>
<link>
<![CDATA[ http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11428259 ]]>
</link>
<description>
<![CDATA[ The realization of a geopotential-based height system relies on high-resolution and high-precision regional gravity field modeling. In this study, a two-cumulative spherical radial basis functions (SRBFs) modeling approach is applied to a developed regional gravity field model, with the goal of realizing a geopotential-based height system in Hong Kong. The traditional one-step SRBF method has difficulty effectively matching the spectral domain and bandwidth of the observations with the target signal. In contrast, this two-cumulative SRBFs approach involves the construction of a high-frequency SRBF model, which can capture more detailed spectral domain signals. Detailed explanations are given regarding the configuration of the SRBFs’ modeling parameters: the location of the SRBFs, the SRBFs type, the edge effect, and the degree of expansion. An edge-constrained estimation model is applied to mitigate the edge effects and enhance the stability of SRBFs coefficient estimation. In addition, we use a prior observation comparison method to determine the optimal maximum and minimum degrees of expansion. The remove–compute–restore (RCR) technology is applied to develop a regional quasi-geoid model in Hong Kong by combining the EIGEN_6C4 model, residual terrain model (RTM), and a two-cumulative SRBFs model. Numerical comparison results reveal that the two-cumulative SRBFs modeling approach is highly consistent with the Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS)/leveling-based quasi-geoid height, and demonstrates superior accuracy with a standard deviation (STD) of 1.8 cm. The two-cumulative SRBFs modeling approach represents an improvement of 9.1% compared to the one-step SRBFs modeling approach. These results indicate the validity of the two-cumulative SRBFs approach for gravity field modeling. Finally, the geopotential value of Hong Kong Principal Datum (HKPD) is estimated to be equal to $62~653~859.83~\pm ~0.18 \ \mathrm{m}^{2} ~\cdot $ s−2 based on the two-cumulative SRBFs model. The vertical offset of HKPD with respect to the global reference potential $W_{0}$ is derived as $- 0.657~\pm 0.018$ m. ]]>
